1
00:00:02,231 --> 00:00:21,280
[SPEAKER_00]: So, recently there was a question about ignorance and how can we uproot ignorance, how can we notice it, you know, during the day and uproot it.

2
00:00:24,037 --> 00:00:44,532
[SPEAKER_00]: I think one of the manifestations of ignorance is distracting ourselves, acts of distraction, turning away from what you feel, turning away from unpleasant things, just trying to go into this oblivious state of

3
00:00:45,707 --> 00:00:48,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but it's pleasant as well.

4
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:53,916
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just kind of... Well, it's not even specifically pleasant.

5
00:00:54,036 --> 00:00:59,563
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like, just seemingly offers you a break from pressure.

6
00:01:00,956 --> 00:01:05,158
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, the pleasure of that indolence is simply determined by how much pressure you are.

7
00:01:05,218 --> 00:01:06,859
[SPEAKER_01]: But often, it's not even pleasant.

8
00:01:07,599 --> 00:01:12,882
[SPEAKER_01]: So people find themselves in a bored state, but then they can't help and just sort of do this thing.

9
00:01:12,902 --> 00:01:14,663
[SPEAKER_01]: They have no pleasure from it anymore.

10
00:01:15,363 --> 00:01:22,127
[SPEAKER_01]: But you still have to distract yourself from it, from other things that are pressuring you unpleasantly.

11
00:01:22,747 --> 00:01:23,988
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you distract yourself.

12
00:01:24,768 --> 00:01:31,291
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason you distract yourself is because it gives you a release from the pressure that you are... Yeah.

13
00:01:31,311 --> 00:01:33,012
[SPEAKER_00]: ...generally feeling.

14
00:01:33,332 --> 00:01:48,920
[SPEAKER_01]: But much like sensuality, it's the same principle, because the release you get from distraction is the release that you get through senses, which is what pressures you in the first place.

15
00:01:51,819 --> 00:01:57,663
[SPEAKER_01]: The senses pressure you, and the only way for you to have a release from that pressure is to give in to the senses more.

16
00:01:59,204 --> 00:02:04,267
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, nothing changes on the level of senses, which is why you're pressured.

17
00:02:04,987 --> 00:02:12,672
[SPEAKER_01]: The only thing that changes, then, is the amount of you actively ignoring the reality of your situation.

18
00:02:14,011 --> 00:02:18,057
[SPEAKER_01]: So, things that are distraction... things themselves are not a distraction.

19
00:02:18,978 --> 00:02:22,603
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your attitude that finds distraction in doing certain things.

20
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:27,923
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, sometimes those things are very different, depending on people.

21
00:02:28,023 --> 00:02:29,044
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not always the same thing.

22
00:02:29,204 --> 00:02:34,968
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, so you... you try to distract yourself from pain...

23
00:02:35,028 --> 00:02:35,549
[SPEAKER_00]: The pressure.

24
00:02:35,649 --> 00:02:36,229
[SPEAKER_00]: The pressure.

25
00:02:36,490 --> 00:02:36,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Not specifically...

26
00:02:37,010 --> 00:02:43,155
[SPEAKER_01]: But through sensuality, so you can actually... Well, that's why... that's why the distraction always leads to further sensuality.

27
00:02:43,355 --> 00:02:43,595
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

28
00:02:44,576 --> 00:02:49,260
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are cultivating the basis of that indolence, you're cultivating the value of sensuality.

29
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:50,821
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all within the central being.

30
00:02:53,860 --> 00:02:55,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's all pertaining to the senses.

31
00:02:56,661 --> 00:03:06,245
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you find a way to relieve yourself of the pressure of the senses without engaging with the senses?

32
00:03:06,865 --> 00:03:12,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Without... and when I say engaging, that's usually what people kind of do to distract themselves.

33
00:03:12,108 --> 00:03:15,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Either find meaningless tasks or even meaningful tasks.

34
00:03:15,950 --> 00:03:25,258
[SPEAKER_01]: It's tasks that have a reason for performing, but you do it for the real reasons of just distracting yourself from yourself, which is too unpleasant, too much of a burden, too much of a pressure.

35
00:03:26,219 --> 00:03:38,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, commonly, people will distract themselves by just, you know, I'm not seeking sensuality specifically, but I'll go out and hang out with friends, just to kill some time, watching movies, entertainment.

36
00:03:38,489 --> 00:03:47,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing like extra sensual, but in itself it's still visual sort of distraction, audible music.

37
00:03:47,337 --> 00:03:52,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Music, you know, many people just constantly have music playing in the background.

38
00:03:53,084 --> 00:03:54,946
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the silence becomes too pressuring.

39
00:03:54,986 --> 00:03:58,489
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you've had, like, you get tired from general working.

40
00:03:58,509 --> 00:03:59,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Tired from work.

41
00:03:59,189 --> 00:04:00,170
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to relax.

42
00:04:00,570 --> 00:04:07,336
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you watch a... so, then you have to switch on a movie, listen to music and be a passive... Yeah, but that's the thing.

43
00:04:07,356 --> 00:04:07,857
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize...

44
00:04:08,057 --> 00:04:09,378
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do you want to relax from?

45
00:04:10,679 --> 00:04:15,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, you're jaded from having to deal with sense input the whole day.

46
00:04:15,843 --> 00:04:16,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

47
00:04:16,104 --> 00:04:17,845
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're physically tired and stuff.

48
00:04:18,085 --> 00:04:20,127
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can simply relax by going to sleep.

49
00:04:22,069 --> 00:04:29,633
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you realize you have a choice there, and you choose to sit for another three hours of enduring the senses and watch a movie.

50
00:04:29,953 --> 00:04:34,695
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't really relax you, because it's not... it hasn't taken you away from the senses, like when you fall asleep.

51
00:04:35,476 --> 00:04:36,636
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what has happened there?

52
00:04:37,057 --> 00:04:41,759
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's just enabled your mind to go into a state of self-ignoring.

53
00:04:41,799 --> 00:04:43,160
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why that's unwholesome.

54
00:04:43,620 --> 00:04:48,202
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, watching a movie is not in itself immoral or not virtuous.

55
00:04:48,322 --> 00:04:49,263
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of irrelevant.

56
00:04:50,368 --> 00:05:01,231
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you do it for the complete reasons, basically, of just self-ignoring, ignoring the state that you are in, that you find then relaxing, that's why it's unwholesome.

57
00:05:01,251 --> 00:05:02,672
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's your reason behind unwholesome.

58
00:05:02,972 --> 00:05:07,493
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're tired from your senses, go to sleep.

59
00:05:07,513 --> 00:05:14,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Not go back to the senses and find something that will just put you in a state of oblivion, while you're still pressured by the same senses.

60
00:05:17,456 --> 00:05:20,518
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's something more dangerous with that as well.

61
00:05:20,718 --> 00:05:30,866
[SPEAKER_01]: There's... Well, it's dangerous because you realize that only true non-pressure relief of that burden is when you're not dependent on the senses.

62
00:05:32,447 --> 00:05:39,793
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you are cultivating the state of ignoring as your form of relief that you know and value, that's very much dependent on the senses.

63
00:05:40,960 --> 00:05:44,543
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that which is the only relief you know, it's not a relief at all.

64
00:05:45,464 --> 00:05:46,585
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's making things worse.

65
00:05:46,885 --> 00:05:47,285
[SPEAKER_01]: And it is.

66
00:05:50,148 --> 00:05:55,252
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, I mean, you then inevitably drift more and more into valuing sensuality.

67
00:06:03,879 --> 00:06:04,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense experience.

68
00:06:04,900 --> 00:06:06,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because you had, again, you had a choice.

69
00:06:06,421 --> 00:06:07,923
[SPEAKER_01]: You couldn't have just went to sleep.

70
00:06:09,192 --> 00:06:15,114
[SPEAKER_01]: But you also have an option to, you know, have visual stimulation that will make you forget about yourself for an hour and a half.

71
00:06:16,654 --> 00:06:17,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Why then...

72
00:06:17,815 --> 00:06:26,097
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if the problem is being pressured by the senses, why is your form of relief forgetting about yourself, which has nothing to do with the senses?

73
00:06:26,157 --> 00:06:27,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that your relief?

74
00:06:28,298 --> 00:06:33,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Surely, if senses are the ones that are pressuring you, your relief should be have a break from the senses.

75
00:06:36,495 --> 00:06:47,363
[SPEAKER_01]: But you kind of know it's senses that are pressuring you, yet then the relief that you have, that you value, that you cultivate, is the relief of ignoring yourself in regard to your senses and don't do anything about the senses.

76
00:06:47,523 --> 00:06:48,484
[SPEAKER_01]: But the senses are the problem.

77
00:06:50,325 --> 00:06:51,386
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why it's dangerous.

78
00:06:51,606 --> 00:06:58,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it not just puts you more into sensuality, but it basically makes you develop even more distorted views of where the problem is.

79
00:07:01,173 --> 00:07:05,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you start valuing that state of self-ignoring, of unawareness.

80
00:07:06,711 --> 00:07:07,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Even when you're not distracted.

81
00:07:08,852 --> 00:07:12,454
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why delusion is the hardest to see and hardest to uproot, the stickiest.

82
00:07:15,015 --> 00:07:18,237
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because you value that, oh, I just deserve to have a break.

83
00:07:18,337 --> 00:07:19,157
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, go and sleep.

84
00:07:19,697 --> 00:07:20,838
[SPEAKER_01]: No, but I want to watch a movie.

85
00:07:20,858 --> 00:07:21,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

86
00:07:21,938 --> 00:07:24,180
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your break means engagement with sensuality.

87
00:07:25,660 --> 00:07:29,522
[SPEAKER_01]: And the only result of that is no change in the level of senses, how much you engage with it.

88
00:07:30,002 --> 00:07:32,444
[SPEAKER_01]: The result is how much you're unaware of yourself.

89
00:07:34,305 --> 00:07:35,165
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're cultivating

90
00:07:35,861 --> 00:07:39,823
[SPEAKER_01]: A habit of unawareness in regard to yourself, in regard to your own mind.

91
00:07:40,963 --> 00:07:46,205
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even as I said, even when you're not necessarily distracting yourself, that is your view now.

92
00:07:46,225 --> 00:07:47,285
[SPEAKER_01]: You value unawareness.

93
00:07:47,305 --> 00:07:49,186
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any opportunity you get, you will be unaware.

94
00:07:50,346 --> 00:07:54,968
[SPEAKER_01]: You're narrowing down the recognition of your own situation.

95
00:07:55,008 --> 00:07:59,870
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's so dangerous and that's why both ill-will and sensuality are rooted in that.

96
00:08:00,995 --> 00:08:02,296
[SPEAKER_01]: That attitude of delusion...

97
00:08:02,316 --> 00:08:04,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Helps me forget my situation.

98
00:08:04,657 --> 00:08:12,521
[SPEAKER_01]: And by forgetting your situation, you then... you cultivate then that kind of basis of forgetfulness in regard to other things that you shouldn't be forgetting.

99
00:08:12,801 --> 00:08:12,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

100
00:08:13,121 --> 00:08:15,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Such as motivation for your actions and so on.

101
00:08:15,862 --> 00:08:16,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

102
00:08:17,663 --> 00:08:26,708
[SPEAKER_00]: So that... building up that kind of habit, you are... the state of your mind that you're developing is this

103
00:08:28,628 --> 00:08:30,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Inactive mind.

104
00:08:30,829 --> 00:08:36,552
[SPEAKER_01]: It is complete, yeah, dull, opaque, obtuse, indolent, inactive, yeah.

105
00:08:36,732 --> 00:08:40,234
[SPEAKER_01]: There is absolutely no, no recognition of thinking going on there.

106
00:08:40,354 --> 00:08:41,995
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no right thinking going on there.

107
00:08:44,196 --> 00:08:45,337
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, that's the whole point.

108
00:08:45,397 --> 00:08:56,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Recognizing that, wait, why, if senses are the one that are making me tired, why do I hold a view that state of unawareness is my relaxation?

109
00:08:57,874 --> 00:08:59,435
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize there's no reason for that view.

110
00:08:59,895 --> 00:09:01,875
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason you hold it is because you never thought about it.

111
00:09:02,576 --> 00:09:11,579
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you do start reflecting upon it and abandon that view, then you realize that absolute, 100% alertness throughout the day cannot make you tired.

112
00:09:12,799 --> 00:09:14,239
[SPEAKER_00]: It has nothing to do with your tiredness.

113
00:09:16,580 --> 00:09:20,822
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, there is never an excuse then to be unmindful and unaware.

114
00:09:21,502 --> 00:09:22,362
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what Buddha would say.

115
00:09:22,382 --> 00:09:24,123
[SPEAKER_01]: You spend the whole day practicing mindfulness.

116
00:09:25,029 --> 00:09:26,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not tiring.

117
00:09:26,650 --> 00:09:28,751
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why he said you can't have too much of mindfulness.

118
00:09:29,772 --> 00:09:34,835
[SPEAKER_01]: You can have too much of pondering, too little pondering, too slack, too tight, but you can't have too much of mindfulness.

119
00:09:35,455 --> 00:09:36,516
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not tiring.

120
00:09:37,457 --> 00:09:39,318
[SPEAKER_01]: The mindfulness... Mindfulness and awareness.

121
00:09:39,418 --> 00:09:40,438
[SPEAKER_01]: So, awareness is not...

122
00:09:40,458 --> 00:09:44,961
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why is then awareness felt like you're... it's tiring you, it's work?

123
00:09:45,802 --> 00:09:47,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you haven't understood it, you're not doing it rightly.

124
00:09:48,769 --> 00:09:54,714
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're not doing it rightly, because you keep cultivating, then, the attitude that the non-work and relaxation is unawareness.

125
00:09:55,415 --> 00:09:58,677
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is no reason for that attitude, except your own habit.

126
00:09:58,817 --> 00:10:05,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, just... just present moment, whatever here and now, don't think about tomorrow, don't think about what happened yesterday.

127
00:10:05,383 --> 00:10:05,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

128
00:10:05,963 --> 00:10:09,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Just hear what you have, just really be with the music.

129
00:10:09,206 --> 00:10:10,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's how mindfulness is, exactly.

130
00:10:10,707 --> 00:10:12,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Be with the movie, be with the... Be with the senses.

131
00:10:12,909 --> 00:10:13,310
[SPEAKER_00]: The sense.

132
00:10:13,830 --> 00:10:14,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Experience now.

133
00:10:14,790 --> 00:10:18,791
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness is recollectiveness of yesterday and what's in store for you tomorrow.

134
00:10:19,051 --> 00:10:21,011
[SPEAKER_01]: At the same time, presently, right here, right now.

135
00:10:21,171 --> 00:10:22,112
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what mindfulness is.

136
00:10:22,512 --> 00:10:23,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Not exclusion of those things.

137
00:10:24,192 --> 00:10:24,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

138
00:10:24,792 --> 00:10:29,733
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the Buddha would say, even when you go to bed, you go bed mindfully with the thought when you wake up.

139
00:10:29,913 --> 00:10:32,073
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you truly switch off from the senses.

140
00:10:33,114 --> 00:10:33,854
[SPEAKER_01]: You go to sleep.

141
00:10:34,854 --> 00:10:35,614
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you wake up.

142
00:10:35,674 --> 00:10:38,475
[SPEAKER_01]: The moment you wake up, you don't need time to pick up your awareness.

143
00:10:38,495 --> 00:10:39,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Your awareness is already there.

144
00:10:41,827 --> 00:10:50,199
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can cultivate that if you don't willingly and consciously choose to go the paths of unawareness and distraction and indolence.

145
00:10:51,341 --> 00:10:52,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Indolence is?

146
00:10:52,643 --> 00:10:54,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Laziness, dullness, indolence.

147
00:10:54,686 --> 00:10:55,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Laziness of mind.

148
00:10:55,627 --> 00:10:56,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, laziness of mind.

149
00:10:56,689 --> 00:10:57,009
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

150
00:10:59,515 --> 00:11:02,696
[SPEAKER_00]: The work of understanding, say, what mindfulness is...

151
00:11:02,896 --> 00:11:05,056
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if the mind is lazy, mind won't be mindful.

152
00:11:05,316 --> 00:11:10,297
[SPEAKER_01]: If the mind is lazy, mind won't have energy to make the effort and reflect upon things that need to be reflected upon.

153
00:11:10,877 --> 00:11:14,498
[SPEAKER_01]: If the mind is lazy, it's not going to even self-interrogate in terms of your motivation stuff.

154
00:11:14,518 --> 00:11:17,559
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just going to go along with the path of least resistance.

155
00:11:17,579 --> 00:11:22,360
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if the mind is lazy, those four right strivings are not present.

156
00:11:23,005 --> 00:11:26,286
[SPEAKER_01]: If the mind is lazy, there are no factors of enlightenment being cultivated.

157
00:11:27,106 --> 00:11:33,388
[SPEAKER_01]: If the mind values laziness and indolence and delusion of that kind, those four energies, four powers, are not there.

158
00:11:34,828 --> 00:11:35,368
[SPEAKER_01]: They can't be.

159
00:11:36,348 --> 00:11:37,929
[SPEAKER_01]: But if the mind is not lazy, so it...

160
00:11:38,329 --> 00:11:44,030
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, initially, you might feel like, oh, if I practice not lazy, you feel all, like, wound up, because you're practicing mindfulness.

161
00:11:44,150 --> 00:11:45,931
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's because you don't know what mindfulness is.

162
00:11:47,291 --> 00:11:50,392
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you work through that, you realize, actually, mindfulness is effortless.

163
00:11:50,967 --> 00:11:52,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Recollectedness is effortless.

164
00:11:52,687 --> 00:11:53,468
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need...

165
00:11:53,488 --> 00:12:03,890
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter how tired, how physically fatigued I am, bombarded by the unpleasant sort of enduring of heat, cold, work, whatever else.

166
00:12:05,010 --> 00:12:09,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Having mind resting has nothing to do with it.

167
00:12:10,092 --> 00:12:14,273
[SPEAKER_01]: It's my own assumption of this is what rest is, completely gratuitous.

168
00:12:14,993 --> 00:12:16,033
[SPEAKER_01]: While in reality then,

169
00:12:17,376 --> 00:12:20,179
[SPEAKER_01]: The only thing that results into is unawareness, nothing else.

170
00:12:20,399 --> 00:12:23,582
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no actual factual, because even after you watch a movie, you have to go and sleep.

171
00:12:24,002 --> 00:12:26,364
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you're really tired.

172
00:12:26,384 --> 00:12:32,809
[SPEAKER_01]: You never really rested, yet you have to go through these motions of what you deem restfulness, so to speak, is, which is centrality.

173
00:12:33,930 --> 00:12:34,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Escape from pain.

174
00:12:36,412 --> 00:12:41,657
[SPEAKER_01]: The only known escape from pain is through seeking pleasure of centrality, and this is like a low-key type of that.

175
00:12:46,256 --> 00:12:47,377
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no benefit there.

176
00:12:48,038 --> 00:12:48,358
[SPEAKER_00]: So... No.

177
00:12:48,398 --> 00:12:48,939
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the opposite.

178
00:12:48,999 --> 00:12:49,199
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

179
00:12:49,299 --> 00:12:50,080
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no benefit.

180
00:12:50,140 --> 00:12:50,761
[SPEAKER_00]: So distracting yourself...

181
00:12:50,801 --> 00:12:51,822
[SPEAKER_00]: It's completely non-beneficial.

182
00:12:51,842 --> 00:12:56,107
[SPEAKER_00]: ...being passive to images on the screen or something.

183
00:12:56,187 --> 00:12:57,608
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if you try to...

184
00:12:59,791 --> 00:13:02,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you try to present, oh, it's... it's...

185
00:13:02,113 --> 00:13:02,914
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

186
00:13:02,934 --> 00:13:05,056
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a documentary or something.

187
00:13:06,483 --> 00:13:07,964
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but is that the real motivation?

188
00:13:08,084 --> 00:13:09,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there a necessity for it?

189
00:13:09,386 --> 00:13:09,526
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

190
00:13:10,006 --> 00:13:11,107
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the motivation for watching it?

191
00:13:11,527 --> 00:13:12,969
[SPEAKER_01]: You need a break.

192
00:13:13,109 --> 00:13:13,769
[SPEAKER_01]: Break from what?

193
00:13:13,990 --> 00:13:14,650
[SPEAKER_01]: From the senses?

194
00:13:14,970 --> 00:13:15,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

195
00:13:15,791 --> 00:13:17,152
[SPEAKER_01]: By engaging more with the senses.

196
00:13:17,392 --> 00:13:21,256
[SPEAKER_01]: How does that make sense?

197
00:13:21,276 --> 00:13:28,182
[SPEAKER_00]: So, having a mind which is active or alert, an alert mind,

198
00:13:30,738 --> 00:13:36,661
[SPEAKER_00]: It's somehow... obviously, if you don't have an alert mind, it's quite painful.

199
00:13:37,202 --> 00:13:38,503
[SPEAKER_00]: It's quite... it's a lot of work.

200
00:13:38,523 --> 00:13:43,125
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a lot of... Yeah, well, that... compared to... basically, you're already in a state of being dead.

201
00:13:43,165 --> 00:13:44,986
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't practice mindfulness, you're as if dead.

202
00:13:45,447 --> 00:13:48,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so it's almost coming back to... Yeah, you're fully... coming to life.

203
00:13:48,188 --> 00:13:49,769
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, coming to life.

204
00:13:49,809 --> 00:13:51,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Escaping death by practicing mindfulness.

205
00:13:52,250 --> 00:13:52,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Recollectedness.

206
00:14:02,178 --> 00:14:03,499
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what the illusion is.

207
00:14:03,519 --> 00:14:08,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Those little things you give in... you give in to throughout the day.

208
00:14:10,165 --> 00:14:11,026
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, I work hard.

209
00:14:11,086 --> 00:14:11,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, you did.

210
00:14:12,327 --> 00:14:12,948
[SPEAKER_01]: I need a break.

211
00:14:13,048 --> 00:14:13,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, you do.

212
00:14:14,429 --> 00:14:16,531
[SPEAKER_01]: How you go about it, that's your choice.

213
00:14:17,172 --> 00:14:18,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you need a break by just sitting quietly?

214
00:14:19,514 --> 00:14:20,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Go to sleep?

215
00:14:21,656 --> 00:14:23,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Or do you need a break by entertaining yourself?

216
00:14:25,179 --> 00:14:25,599
[SPEAKER_01]: With what?

217
00:14:25,639 --> 00:14:28,142
[SPEAKER_01]: With the senses that made you tired in the first place.

218
00:14:32,907 --> 00:14:41,952
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in terms of making the mind strong, making the mind alert, fully active, aware, constantly.

219
00:14:43,392 --> 00:14:45,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Precepts, virtues, the first thing, of course.

220
00:14:46,774 --> 00:14:47,455
[SPEAKER_01]: And then is that?

221
00:14:48,155 --> 00:14:51,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to understand correctly what that effortless mindfulness is.

222
00:14:53,242 --> 00:14:56,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're doing mindfulness with effort, it's not the mindfulness you should be doing.

223
00:14:57,263 --> 00:14:59,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, it's not the mindfulness that needs to be cultivated.

224
00:14:59,524 --> 00:15:15,587
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not going to start perfectly right off the bat, but if you know that you need to find the mindfulness that is effortless, that doesn't depend on the senses and what you attend to and so on, then you will be seeking it.

225
00:15:15,607 --> 00:15:17,948
[SPEAKER_01]: So, trial and error, you can arrive at it eventually.

226
00:15:19,883 --> 00:15:20,203
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's...

227
00:15:21,004 --> 00:15:31,370
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you are tired, burdened, you know, physically tired, physically burdened, enduring elements, none of that would be mentally tiring, because none of that is mentally tiring.

228
00:15:33,252 --> 00:15:36,874
[SPEAKER_01]: You make it mentally tiring by wanting to practice an awareness of it.

229
00:15:38,755 --> 00:15:46,220
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's say, one is contemplating dhamma, one is thinking about an aspect, but it's...

230
00:15:47,878 --> 00:15:49,979
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of... you have to put effort in your thinking.

231
00:15:50,319 --> 00:15:52,099
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to put some energy there.

232
00:15:52,739 --> 00:15:54,420
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's... that's not effort.

233
00:15:54,620 --> 00:16:00,741
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't have... you can't expect that type of thinking or figuring... investigation to happen effortlessly.

234
00:16:01,481 --> 00:16:02,322
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll watch my breath.

235
00:16:02,582 --> 00:16:03,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, sure.

236
00:16:03,302 --> 00:16:05,602
[SPEAKER_00]: And then contemplation will magically occur.

237
00:16:05,762 --> 00:16:07,763
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to understand... Oh, absolutely not.

238
00:16:07,803 --> 00:16:15,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, all those notions that... that many people would have of such practices would be dispelled if they were just to start reading suttas.

239
00:16:16,525 --> 00:16:18,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, more systematically, just read the actual suttas.

240
00:16:19,547 --> 00:16:30,571
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, on many occasions, the Buddha would say himself, like, there's that famous sutta, when he said, he said, oh, what if, what if I were to not think any unwholesome thought and only think, make effort to think wholesome thoughts?

241
00:16:30,952 --> 00:16:35,714
[SPEAKER_01]: And he spent day and night just thinking right things and not thinking the bad things.

242
00:16:36,374 --> 00:16:38,395
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, tireless energy arose in me.

243
00:16:39,729 --> 00:16:41,250
[SPEAKER_01]: He could think only what he wants.

244
00:16:41,390 --> 00:16:45,114
[SPEAKER_01]: So he was like, through work, built up that energy momentum.

245
00:16:45,774 --> 00:16:52,840
[SPEAKER_01]: And when he purified his thinking, nothing unwholesome would be entertained, welcomed, maintained, encouraged.

246
00:16:53,461 --> 00:16:54,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Only wholesome.

247
00:16:54,922 --> 00:16:58,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he says, okay, what if I were to now calm myself down?

248
00:17:00,381 --> 00:17:04,562
[SPEAKER_01]: But not by not thinking or not paying attention to whatever I think it's fine.

249
00:17:05,122 --> 00:17:07,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Simply, how do I take this step further?

250
00:17:07,402 --> 00:17:08,363
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the first jhāna.

251
00:17:08,643 --> 00:17:14,604
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking and pondering, that now you develop calmness on account of.

252
00:17:14,644 --> 00:17:21,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Not through cancellation of your thoughts, but through basically rising up, finding what calmness is within the thinking mind.

253
00:17:22,005 --> 00:17:24,366
[SPEAKER_01]: But first, you need to develop the thinking mind correctly.

254
00:17:27,027 --> 00:17:31,928
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, that's what I mean, like, that energy is a, it's, it's a must.

255
00:17:32,349 --> 00:17:34,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Effort, repetition, drilling, training.

256
00:17:35,009 --> 00:17:44,952
[SPEAKER_01]: And I said that probably before, but I remember when I started it in the suttas as a layman, and, you know, you have all these notions, and then the Buddha kept talking about the training.

257
00:17:44,992 --> 00:17:46,053
[SPEAKER_01]: He trains himself thus.

258
00:17:46,073 --> 00:17:47,113
[SPEAKER_01]: He trains himself thus.

259
00:17:47,533 --> 00:17:51,154
[SPEAKER_01]: And in the beginning, I remember, I couldn't quite, like, what is there to train?

260
00:17:51,194 --> 00:17:54,195
[SPEAKER_01]: You either become enlightened, or you're not enlightened.

261
00:17:55,153 --> 00:18:01,795
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you might train indirectly, like you keep the precepts, you do the right things, but then enlightenment happens to you.

262
00:18:01,835 --> 00:18:04,455
[SPEAKER_01]: But how do you train the enlightenment?

263
00:18:04,495 --> 00:18:05,376
[SPEAKER_01]: It did not make sense.

264
00:18:06,296 --> 00:18:08,737
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you read the suttas more and more and say, no, that's what enlightenment is.

265
00:18:09,517 --> 00:18:11,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Rising above the unenlightening things.

266
00:18:12,457 --> 00:18:17,559
[SPEAKER_01]: And the only way you can rise above it is through manly effort, manly strength, manly determination, manly training.

267
00:18:17,839 --> 00:18:18,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Manly contemplation.

268
00:18:18,919 --> 00:18:22,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Manly contemplation, manly perseverance, manly patience.

269
00:18:22,931 --> 00:18:26,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever I was trying to talk about, the similes of the warrior and so on, it's not poetic.

270
00:18:27,794 --> 00:18:30,055
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's making an effort in investigation.

271
00:18:30,495 --> 00:18:30,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

272
00:18:31,035 --> 00:18:33,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like I'm blindly making effort in focusing on my nostrils.

273
00:18:33,997 --> 00:18:34,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

274
00:18:34,337 --> 00:18:35,378
[SPEAKER_01]: And what's the result of that?

275
00:18:36,498 --> 00:18:36,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

276
00:18:37,059 --> 00:18:37,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

277
00:18:37,279 --> 00:18:37,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

278
00:18:37,739 --> 00:18:38,319
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the point?

279
00:18:38,339 --> 00:18:39,380
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about understanding.

280
00:18:39,740 --> 00:18:41,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Freedom comes out of understanding.

281
00:18:42,262 --> 00:18:44,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Full understanding is what arahantship is.

282
00:18:44,643 --> 00:18:45,803
[SPEAKER_01]: So, am I understanding?

283
00:18:46,224 --> 00:18:46,904
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

284
00:18:46,944 --> 00:18:48,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I making effort?

285
00:18:48,045 --> 00:18:48,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

286
00:18:49,946 --> 00:18:50,647
[SPEAKER_01]: To understand.

287
00:18:51,167 --> 00:18:53,048
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, am I clearly thinking towards understanding?

288
00:18:53,588 --> 00:18:53,728
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

289
00:18:53,828 --> 00:19:01,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, then, I can know for safely, then, that I don't understand, but I will not understand for sure, if I don't at least try to understand.

290
00:19:02,412 --> 00:19:04,593
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do I then go about trying to understand?

291
00:19:04,673 --> 00:19:05,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

292
00:19:05,633 --> 00:19:08,074
[SPEAKER_00]: How does that manifest, then, as practice?

293
00:19:08,094 --> 00:19:09,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Read the suttas.

294
00:19:09,795 --> 00:19:10,255
[SPEAKER_01]: A lot of it.

295
00:19:10,355 --> 00:19:13,897
[SPEAKER_01]: And not just read it, and then get inspired on account of it.

296
00:19:15,235 --> 00:19:19,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, you know, might... will probably happen inevitably, but don't take that to be the goal.

297
00:19:19,618 --> 00:19:20,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, great.

298
00:19:20,258 --> 00:19:21,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you're inspired.

299
00:19:21,359 --> 00:19:24,621
[SPEAKER_01]: Now go back to the same sutta that inspired you so much and understand it.

300
00:19:25,121 --> 00:19:35,028
[SPEAKER_01]: And as I said many times before, you will know if you're understanding the meaning of the dhamma that the Buddha laid out, if you're becoming incapable to suffer amidst suffering.

301
00:19:35,588 --> 00:19:37,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Not if you don't suffer at the moment.

302
00:19:39,531 --> 00:19:42,273
[SPEAKER_01]: If the worst thing were to happen to you, how much would you suffer?

303
00:19:42,633 --> 00:19:46,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Before you understood what you understood, and now after you understood it.

304
00:19:46,555 --> 00:19:47,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Has that suffering diminished?

305
00:19:48,257 --> 00:19:49,417
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can have a sense of it.

306
00:19:50,398 --> 00:19:56,622
[SPEAKER_01]: You might not... you will not know exactly how much has diminished, but you will have a sense that I am less touched by suffering.

307
00:19:57,022 --> 00:19:57,883
[SPEAKER_01]: There is less pain.

308
00:19:58,563 --> 00:20:01,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because I'm running away, justifying, psychologizing, trying to avoid it.

309
00:20:02,225 --> 00:20:04,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Simply because, like, it cannot touch me.

310
00:20:06,529 --> 00:20:08,211
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how you become fearless as well.

311
00:20:08,872 --> 00:20:11,394
[SPEAKER_01]: A person who doesn't experience fear is not fearless.

312
00:20:12,676 --> 00:20:12,836
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

313
00:20:13,617 --> 00:20:19,804
[SPEAKER_01]: A person who, within, like, within the experience of fear, doesn't fear it, he's a fearless one.

314
00:20:19,844 --> 00:20:26,711
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're constantly hiding or frightening, away from frightening things, you don't know if you're fearless or not, because you're just hiding from fear.

315
00:20:26,791 --> 00:20:27,933
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you know if you're fearless?

316
00:20:29,631 --> 00:20:34,693
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just fear-free at the time, but you're not fearless, as in incapable of being afraid.

317
00:20:35,994 --> 00:20:50,360
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a completely different thing, that you can only develop through understanding and overcoming fear, existential fear, fear of restraint, fear of giving things up, fear of losing your possessions, your attachments, your loved ones.

318
00:20:51,841 --> 00:20:54,622
[SPEAKER_01]: So, Arahant, he's the fearless one, incapable of being afraid.

319
00:20:55,753 --> 00:20:57,015
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not brave.

320
00:20:57,075 --> 00:20:57,716
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not brave.

321
00:20:58,497 --> 00:21:00,079
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

322
00:21:00,159 --> 00:21:01,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Bravery, you need the fear.

323
00:21:01,961 --> 00:21:03,123
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, just go against it.

324
00:21:04,986 --> 00:21:09,111
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on a practical level,

325
00:21:10,373 --> 00:21:14,136
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to be trying to understand the teachings of the Buddha.

326
00:21:14,736 --> 00:21:15,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

327
00:21:15,497 --> 00:21:16,918
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the beginning, it's going to be hard.

328
00:21:16,938 --> 00:21:20,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, say, when you learn any... you're learning any new subject or something.

329
00:21:20,500 --> 00:21:24,543
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to read the suttas, practice virtue and precepts.

330
00:21:24,583 --> 00:21:33,770
[SPEAKER_01]: The greater your virtues are, basically, the greater potential for understanding you have.

331
00:21:34,751 --> 00:21:37,012
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you start reading the suttas, and you will.

332
00:21:37,092 --> 00:21:38,914
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start making effort to understand them,

333
00:21:40,029 --> 00:21:54,619
[SPEAKER_01]: And keep reading, and your mind will get hooked onto different aspects that will make more sense, that you think about more through, and clarify them, then suddenly new aspects become revealed, then you go into them, then you go into them, and that should be your meditation as well.

334
00:21:54,960 --> 00:22:04,246
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, before I was enlightened, while I was still unenlightened bodhisattva, I decided, what if I were to think only wholesome thoughts, and not think unwholesome thoughts?

335
00:22:05,507 --> 00:22:08,469
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're a patujana,

336
00:22:09,790 --> 00:22:19,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Not even near the right view, far from arahantship, that is the only thing you should be doing, trying to understand the dhamma.

337
00:22:19,996 --> 00:22:26,200
[SPEAKER_01]: Any meditation you do, concentration, relaxation practice, has nothing to do with the dhamma, because you have no dhamma.

338
00:22:29,041 --> 00:22:32,263
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, again, without the right view, there is no meditation.

339
00:22:32,323 --> 00:22:37,026
[SPEAKER_01]: So how can you meditate, if you don't know what meditation is?

340
00:22:40,230 --> 00:22:41,791
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I mean in the other talks.

341
00:22:41,971 --> 00:22:44,893
[SPEAKER_00]: We covered it about meditation centrality.

342
00:22:44,913 --> 00:22:45,633
[SPEAKER_00]: I forgot the title.

343
00:22:46,574 --> 00:22:47,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that's where you are.

344
00:22:47,414 --> 00:22:54,198
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason you want this meditation, your meditation technique, they make you feel all right and peaceful and so on, is because you want pleasure.

345
00:22:55,078 --> 00:22:57,179
[SPEAKER_01]: You want agreeable experience.

346
00:22:57,260 --> 00:22:57,680
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all.

347
00:22:59,081 --> 00:23:03,183
[SPEAKER_01]: But which means you're choosing to not do the work of getting the right view.

348
00:23:10,520 --> 00:23:11,881
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how you practically go about it.

349
00:23:12,983 --> 00:23:21,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, if sometimes thinking gets like... it's just too much, so to speak, yeah, okay, relax a bit.

350
00:23:22,380 --> 00:23:26,661
[SPEAKER_01]: But don't start valuing that relaxation from work, because you haven't done the necessary work.

351
00:23:26,741 --> 00:23:31,042
[SPEAKER_00]: There's all these things you can do, like, if you read a sutta and it says, you know, understand the body.

352
00:23:31,142 --> 00:23:32,782
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question, what is the body?

353
00:23:33,242 --> 00:23:33,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Answer it.

354
00:23:33,762 --> 00:23:36,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, all of that, all of that will become more apparent once you...

355
00:23:36,563 --> 00:23:38,543
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm talking about the absolute beginner who comes to it.

356
00:23:38,583 --> 00:23:39,164
[SPEAKER_01]: What do I do?

357
00:23:39,984 --> 00:23:44,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you start reading the suttas, you start getting a sense, and then you can have your own interrogations of the things that matter.

358
00:23:44,825 --> 00:23:45,785
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to constantly

359
00:23:46,305 --> 00:23:48,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Be kind of reinvigorated with the suttas.

360
00:23:48,986 --> 00:23:49,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

361
00:23:49,606 --> 00:23:51,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, yeah, body, feeling, feeling, feeling.

362
00:23:51,886 --> 00:23:52,566
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the feeling?

363
00:23:53,046 --> 00:23:53,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling.

364
00:23:53,346 --> 00:23:58,387
[SPEAKER_01]: When I say feeling to myself or to somebody else, what am I referring to?

365
00:23:58,648 --> 00:24:00,288
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, we've done those interrogations.

366
00:24:00,348 --> 00:24:10,410
[SPEAKER_00]: So, someone might read the sutta about, okay, feeling this, feelings this, body that, and read the sutta, and then just stay at that level of

367
00:24:11,052 --> 00:24:12,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, very nice, very nice.

368
00:24:12,213 --> 00:24:12,993
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, inspiration.

369
00:24:13,033 --> 00:24:17,896
[SPEAKER_00]: But that other level of... that needs... there needs to be effort made.

370
00:24:18,136 --> 00:24:18,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

371
00:24:18,337 --> 00:24:19,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is painful.

372
00:24:19,357 --> 00:24:19,958
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the work.

373
00:24:20,038 --> 00:24:21,278
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the work.

374
00:24:21,298 --> 00:24:22,159
[SPEAKER_00]: What does this mean?

375
00:24:22,479 --> 00:24:22,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

376
00:24:22,739 --> 00:24:23,780
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the meaning here?

377
00:24:23,820 --> 00:24:26,682
[SPEAKER_01]: That's very unpleasant, especially if you're not trained in doing it.

378
00:24:26,882 --> 00:24:28,543
[SPEAKER_01]: I'd rather... And most people are not trained in doing it.

379
00:24:28,583 --> 00:24:30,644
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd rather just read the sutta nicely.

380
00:24:31,044 --> 00:24:32,966
[SPEAKER_00]: Get a nice feeling from it.

381
00:24:32,986 --> 00:24:33,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, okay.

382
00:24:33,366 --> 00:24:33,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

383
00:24:33,646 --> 00:24:34,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Four Noble Truths.

384
00:24:34,106 --> 00:24:34,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Same.

385
00:24:34,647 --> 00:24:36,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Same with all these talks and stuff.

386
00:24:36,288 --> 00:24:36,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

387
00:24:36,668 --> 00:24:38,429
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, we try to make our talks

388
00:24:40,128 --> 00:24:51,273
[SPEAKER_01]: As as kind of engaging as possible, but I'm sure many people would just be inspired by it and, you know, inspired by the energy we put out or whatever, I don't know, and not actually... All the style.

389
00:24:51,533 --> 00:24:58,836
[SPEAKER_01]: All the style, the way we speak, I don't know, but be that as it may, you need to understand what's being said.

390
00:24:59,256 --> 00:25:03,278
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're just running off on an inspiration, you're not doing the work.

391
00:25:04,244 --> 00:25:06,826
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's even worse, because you think you're doing the work.

392
00:25:07,226 --> 00:25:11,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, before, at least, you knew you were not doing the work, and you're not doing the work.

393
00:25:12,190 --> 00:25:18,215
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, you think you're doing the work simply by being inspired, by not making effort to understand, while you're not doing the work.

394
00:25:18,896 --> 00:25:22,338
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, it's going to be twice as hard to snap out of that.

395
00:25:23,740 --> 00:25:26,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, by the pretext of doing work, you feel great, inspired.

396
00:25:27,523 --> 00:25:30,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Dhamma is so easy, or so inspiring.

397
00:25:30,405 --> 00:25:32,947
[SPEAKER_01]: But, okay, so are you free from suffering on account of it?

398
00:25:34,717 --> 00:25:36,178
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's no understanding taking place.

399
00:25:36,518 --> 00:25:37,699
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what are you inspired about?

400
00:25:38,840 --> 00:25:41,261
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm inspired that it gives me a nice feeling.

401
00:25:41,281 --> 00:25:42,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Gives me a nice feeling, yeah.

402
00:25:43,302 --> 00:25:44,303
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm inspired with pleasure.

403
00:25:44,943 --> 00:25:45,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Same thing.

404
00:25:46,304 --> 00:25:52,727
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's inevitable to experience that when you either read the suttas or listen to a dhamma talk by somebody.

405
00:25:53,387 --> 00:25:56,249
[SPEAKER_01]: You're either going to approve or disapprove on that level of your feeling.

406
00:25:57,208 --> 00:26:00,072
[SPEAKER_01]: But if that's your... that's as far as you go...

407
00:26:00,172 --> 00:26:01,034
[SPEAKER_00]: Your criteria...

408
00:26:01,154 --> 00:26:04,078
[SPEAKER_01]: If that's your sole criteria, you're missing out either way.

409
00:26:04,879 --> 00:26:09,686
[SPEAKER_01]: And same, by the way, when people disapprove what's being said, not because of what's being said, it's just how it's being said.

410
00:26:11,758 --> 00:26:14,820
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you clearly don't make an effort to understand what you're saying.

411
00:26:14,840 --> 00:26:17,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Someone might listen to this and say, why is this wrong?

412
00:26:17,402 --> 00:26:18,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I think it's wrong?

413
00:26:18,883 --> 00:26:19,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it right?

414
00:26:19,763 --> 00:26:19,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

415
00:26:20,244 --> 00:26:20,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

416
00:26:20,564 --> 00:26:21,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I tell?

417
00:26:22,345 --> 00:26:23,206
[SPEAKER_00]: Explain why it's right?

418
00:26:23,226 --> 00:26:24,006
[SPEAKER_01]: This ajan is right.

419
00:26:24,406 --> 00:26:24,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

420
00:26:25,107 --> 00:26:25,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he's cool.

421
00:26:26,188 --> 00:26:26,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

422
00:26:26,468 --> 00:26:26,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

423
00:26:26,788 --> 00:26:27,188
[SPEAKER_01]: But why?

424
00:26:27,268 --> 00:26:28,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that right?

425
00:26:28,669 --> 00:26:29,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, I don't like this.

426
00:26:29,650 --> 00:26:30,210
[SPEAKER_01]: This is wrong.

427
00:26:30,310 --> 00:26:30,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, why?

428
00:26:31,171 --> 00:26:32,011
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to know why.

429
00:26:32,632 --> 00:26:33,933
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the important part.

430
00:26:33,953 --> 00:26:34,693
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the work.

431
00:26:34,793 --> 00:26:35,974
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the part.

432
00:26:36,154 --> 00:26:36,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

433
00:26:38,476 --> 00:26:39,476
[SPEAKER_01]: And I remember when I was...

434
00:26:41,647 --> 00:26:50,153
[SPEAKER_01]: I would take up random books from the library, philosophy books and so on, of clearly things that would be regarded as wrong views.

435
00:26:52,395 --> 00:26:59,920
[SPEAKER_01]: But I would read it to understand why it's wrong, not just to get off on the righteousness that this is wrong, this is ridiculously wrong.

436
00:27:00,620 --> 00:27:02,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but I need to see why it is wrong.

437
00:27:04,363 --> 00:27:09,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just, oh, it's wrong because I took it because somebody else that I believe in said so.

438
00:27:10,405 --> 00:27:11,405
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not Buddhist or something.

439
00:27:11,726 --> 00:27:17,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, they were like reading in the Veda and then he would mention all these other philosophers and they were wrong and so on, but he wouldn't dwell on it.

440
00:27:18,188 --> 00:27:21,269
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I found some of those books and I was like, okay, so I need to see why this is wrong.

441
00:27:22,709 --> 00:27:22,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

442
00:27:24,730 --> 00:27:25,690
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to see for myself.

443
00:27:25,890 --> 00:27:27,471
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't just accept that this is wrong.

444
00:27:29,035 --> 00:27:33,478
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, consequently, things that you regard as right, you start putting in question.

445
00:27:33,498 --> 00:27:34,539
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the unpleasant bit.

446
00:27:36,260 --> 00:27:39,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, things that I thought of, you know, I understood.

447
00:27:39,722 --> 00:27:40,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but why?

448
00:27:40,523 --> 00:27:41,203
[SPEAKER_01]: How is this right?

449
00:27:41,344 --> 00:27:42,064
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is this right?

450
00:27:43,265 --> 00:27:45,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it right because it's absolutely contradiction-free?

451
00:27:46,987 --> 00:27:47,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe.

452
00:27:47,608 --> 00:27:48,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, maybe.

453
00:27:48,108 --> 00:27:48,488
[SPEAKER_01]: There you go.

454
00:27:48,508 --> 00:27:49,369
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're doubting it.

455
00:27:49,509 --> 00:27:50,050
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not sure.

456
00:27:50,410 --> 00:27:51,611
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you know it's right?

457
00:27:51,631 --> 00:27:53,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, here you are acting as if you know it's right.

458
00:27:54,895 --> 00:27:55,695
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the practice.

459
00:27:55,915 --> 00:28:00,056
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not coming... it's only partially coming across the right instruction.

460
00:28:00,076 --> 00:28:09,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sure, suttas, talks, but if you never actually start digging things up on that level, internally, making that effort, all of this is meaningless.

461
00:28:09,918 --> 00:28:11,498
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the yonisamanasikāra part.

462
00:28:12,218 --> 00:28:13,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Going back to that origin.

463
00:28:15,779 --> 00:28:20,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Origin of your views with which you are listening to this teaching.

464
00:28:21,249 --> 00:28:23,030
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to then start doing it rightly.

465
00:28:23,070 --> 00:28:25,151
[SPEAKER_00]: What is your perspective consist of?

466
00:28:25,171 --> 00:28:25,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Perspective, yeah.

467
00:28:25,932 --> 00:28:26,912
[SPEAKER_00]: What does it consist of?

468
00:28:27,092 --> 00:28:27,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

469
00:28:27,713 --> 00:28:28,613
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it based on?

470
00:28:28,673 --> 00:28:29,534
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it based upon?

471
00:28:29,774 --> 00:28:31,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Wait a minute.

472
00:28:31,095 --> 00:28:36,117
[SPEAKER_01]: I have this perspective on this, but on account of what I have such a perspective?

473
00:28:36,137 --> 00:28:39,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you realize, more often than not, in account of I'm finding it pleasing.

474
00:28:39,940 --> 00:28:43,061
[SPEAKER_01]: The idea of it, this, whatever, pleases me.

475
00:28:43,261 --> 00:28:44,462
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I assume it is right.

476
00:28:45,042 --> 00:28:48,825
[SPEAKER_01]: I told you that, again, when I was a layman, I think probably my first year of reading the suttas.

477
00:28:48,905 --> 00:28:49,245
[SPEAKER_01]: It was that

478
00:28:50,558 --> 00:28:58,783
[SPEAKER_01]: In Digha Nikāi, I think, Samanyapāla Sutta, it was a sutta that lists about those six different wrongs.

479
00:28:59,044 --> 00:29:01,385
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no, no, no, no.

480
00:29:01,525 --> 00:29:02,446
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it was Samanyapāla.

481
00:29:02,546 --> 00:29:03,827
[SPEAKER_01]: It was the fruits of the holy life.

482
00:29:04,487 --> 00:29:15,935
[SPEAKER_01]: And in that sutta, the Buddha describes these different, like, fundamental other different teachings, six different teachings.

483
00:29:17,888 --> 00:29:23,251
[SPEAKER_01]: About, you know, like, there is nobody getting killed, and then the simile of cutting the sword goes through the arm, and then there's nothing given.

484
00:29:23,271 --> 00:29:23,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Determinism.

485
00:29:23,912 --> 00:29:24,632
[SPEAKER_01]: Determinism.

486
00:29:25,132 --> 00:29:28,394
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't know much about it at the time, and I was reading that sutta.

487
00:29:28,434 --> 00:29:33,117
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a long sutta, so by the time I arrived at that bit, I was reading suttas before that, so by the time I arrived at that sutta,

488
00:29:35,020 --> 00:29:41,168
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't read the leading-up sentence when the Buddha started celebrating on this view that said, these are the wrong views.

489
00:29:41,608 --> 00:29:42,710
[SPEAKER_01]: I kind of...

490
00:29:42,730 --> 00:29:46,875
[SPEAKER_01]: So I just started reading these suttas and I thought the Buddha was teaching that.

491
00:29:47,656 --> 00:29:48,016
[SPEAKER_01]: And then...

492
00:29:49,452 --> 00:29:51,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course, there is nobody killed, because anatta, you know.

493
00:29:52,653 --> 00:29:59,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Then the next one, like the... what was the other one that... that...

494
00:29:59,656 --> 00:30:01,396
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it was that... yeah, determinism, wasn't it?

495
00:30:01,416 --> 00:30:06,878
[SPEAKER_01]: The boiler that will kind of run its course, when you deplete the bad karma in itself, by itself.

496
00:30:06,918 --> 00:30:08,559
[SPEAKER_01]: But I was like, yeah, of course, of course.

497
00:30:08,879 --> 00:30:13,661
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I was reading these views, I was accepting all of them, thinking the Buddha is saying that.

498
00:30:14,401 --> 00:30:19,323
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when he finished, he said, see, that's why these views are wrong, lead to peril.

499
00:30:19,523 --> 00:30:21,263
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was like, oh my goodness.

500
00:30:22,964 --> 00:30:27,345
[SPEAKER_01]: I realized even the stuff that I thought I understood about the Buddha and the teaching of the dhamma.

501
00:30:27,765 --> 00:30:33,347
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't, because I just literally accepted six different wrong views as one, as the dhamma.

502
00:30:34,907 --> 00:30:38,728
[SPEAKER_01]: But fortunately, that concern, I took on as a challenge, to understand it.

503
00:30:38,828 --> 00:30:43,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Not as, oh, I must cover this up and never read this sutta again, and just focus on things I think I understood.

504
00:30:43,829 --> 00:30:43,989
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

505
00:30:44,529 --> 00:30:45,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that determinism wrong?

506
00:30:46,329 --> 00:30:51,510
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's just this huge weight of opaque... you have no idea where to start.

507
00:30:52,510 --> 00:30:53,670
[SPEAKER_01]: I have no idea why it's wrong.

508
00:30:54,530 --> 00:30:56,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I better start making effort to find out.

509
00:30:57,611 --> 00:30:58,451
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is wrong.

510
00:31:00,992 --> 00:31:01,652
[SPEAKER_01]: But it made sense.

511
00:31:01,692 --> 00:31:02,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Anatta.

512
00:31:02,612 --> 00:31:03,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody gets killed.

513
00:31:04,929 --> 00:31:05,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

514
00:31:05,189 --> 00:31:05,609
[SPEAKER_01]: What's wrong?

515
00:31:05,629 --> 00:31:07,811
[SPEAKER_01]: All religions teach the same, just in a different way.

516
00:31:08,511 --> 00:31:09,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Already enlightened.

517
00:31:09,511 --> 00:31:10,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

518
00:31:10,212 --> 00:31:12,053
[SPEAKER_01]: It is like, actually, no, the Buddha said this is wrong.

519
00:31:12,113 --> 00:31:13,414
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I do...

520
00:31:13,634 --> 00:31:29,362
[SPEAKER_01]: I had... and again, fortunately, I had enough faith, even at that initial stage, in the suttas and the Buddha, to accept that, which I clearly did not see as wrong, that it's wrong, but not stay at that level, it's wrong, it's wrong, it's wrong, but it's something that needs to be understood.

521
00:31:30,439 --> 00:31:42,648
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if by understanding, as you're saying earlier, understanding different wrong views, you can uproot those wrong views that you... Yeah, you're getting clearer to what the right view is.

522
00:31:43,569 --> 00:31:48,072
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a different way of progressing, but equally valid, or something you can do both, pretty much.

523
00:31:49,753 --> 00:31:52,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Because all the views you have, as a praducian, are wrong views.

524
00:31:52,855 --> 00:31:57,498
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by undoing them, means by understanding them, you can arrive at the right one.

525
00:31:57,978 --> 00:31:59,399
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not optional to go through that.

526
00:31:59,479 --> 00:32:02,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Just some people need to do more of that, some people need to do less of that.

527
00:32:02,361 --> 00:32:04,262
[SPEAKER_01]: But the work needs to be done.

528
00:32:04,403 --> 00:32:05,023
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the point.

529
00:32:05,523 --> 00:32:05,964
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

530
00:32:05,984 --> 00:32:09,026
[SPEAKER_00]: You have, yeah, you have wrong views, but you don't even know what they are.

531
00:32:09,166 --> 00:32:09,726
[SPEAKER_00]: It's necessary.

532
00:32:09,746 --> 00:32:10,486
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know what...

533
00:32:11,487 --> 00:32:20,435
[SPEAKER_01]: And even, you know, we have the examples in the suttas of people practicing out of faith, you know, the faith type, sadhana, sadhana, and so on.

534
00:32:21,195 --> 00:32:23,517
[SPEAKER_01]: And people often, oh, I'll just focus on devotion.

535
00:32:23,758 --> 00:32:24,879
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not what faith is.

536
00:32:25,419 --> 00:32:30,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Faith is that, accepting that the Buddha is right, even if you have no factual proof for it.

537
00:32:30,543 --> 00:32:31,164
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your faith.

538
00:32:32,505 --> 00:32:40,267
[SPEAKER_01]: But that faith is relevant and valid only if now, on account of that faith, you start doing the same effort.

539
00:32:40,507 --> 00:32:43,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Somebody can't do that just on account of faith.

540
00:32:43,588 --> 00:32:48,870
[SPEAKER_01]: He needs to start making more effort to even arrive at the point to do the effort on account of what the Buddha said.

541
00:32:50,365 --> 00:32:53,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people just hear what the Buddha said and they'll do it.

542
00:32:53,186 --> 00:32:54,266
[SPEAKER_01]: But either way, you need to do it.

543
00:32:54,926 --> 00:33:01,368
[SPEAKER_01]: But these days, when you say faith, I'm a faith type and so on, just means basically bhakti yoga and your devotional worship.

544
00:33:01,968 --> 00:33:04,049
[SPEAKER_01]: No, there's nothing to do with faith that Buddha was teaching.

545
00:33:04,609 --> 00:33:11,611
[SPEAKER_01]: Faith in a doctor, faith in following a doctor prescription without comparing it to other doctors and so on, which some people need to do.

546
00:33:13,131 --> 00:33:15,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, the work needs to be done.

547
00:33:15,332 --> 00:33:16,672
[SPEAKER_01]: That effort needs to be made.

548
00:33:17,573 --> 00:33:18,893
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whichever type you are,

549
00:33:19,620 --> 00:33:20,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

550
00:33:20,490 --> 00:33:21,521
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is the effort.

551
00:33:30,881 --> 00:33:34,944
[SPEAKER_00]: You spoke about it a long time ago, about that mental relaxation again.

552
00:33:36,045 --> 00:33:37,446
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what you must watch out for.

553
00:33:37,906 --> 00:33:38,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Yep.

554
00:33:39,287 --> 00:33:41,069
[SPEAKER_00]: You never want to give in to that.

555
00:33:41,089 --> 00:33:41,369
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

556
00:33:41,389 --> 00:33:43,571
[SPEAKER_00]: That mental indolence.

557
00:33:43,871 --> 00:33:45,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

558
00:33:45,492 --> 00:33:48,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me just put my feet up for five minutes and not think about anything.

559
00:33:49,055 --> 00:33:50,015
[SPEAKER_00]: Find another way.

560
00:33:50,376 --> 00:33:51,096
[SPEAKER_01]: That's unawareness.

561
00:33:52,137 --> 00:33:53,338
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's something you give in to.

562
00:33:53,378 --> 00:33:53,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

563
00:33:53,758 --> 00:33:55,159
[SPEAKER_01]: So, see, I'm physically tired.

564
00:33:55,179 --> 00:33:55,840
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want to think anymore.

565
00:33:55,900 --> 00:33:56,760
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm physically tired.

566
00:33:56,961 --> 00:33:59,522
[SPEAKER_01]: We've been walking 30 kilometers.

567
00:34:00,605 --> 00:34:02,727
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to sit down, put this bag down.

568
00:34:03,047 --> 00:34:03,888
[SPEAKER_01]: That's absolutely fine.

569
00:34:04,028 --> 00:34:06,410
[SPEAKER_01]: You sit down, put the bag down, even go to sleep.

570
00:34:07,651 --> 00:34:08,511
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's where that ends.

571
00:34:09,612 --> 00:34:15,557
[SPEAKER_01]: You choosing, on account of that, to give to unawareness, that's a completely unrelated thing, for which you're responsible.

572
00:34:15,617 --> 00:34:19,020
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where delusion, same as with desire and so on, it's on you.

573
00:34:19,460 --> 00:34:20,681
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not on the maras domain.

574
00:34:20,701 --> 00:34:21,642
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not on the senses.

575
00:34:21,682 --> 00:34:22,803
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not on how tired you are.

576
00:34:22,843 --> 00:34:23,784
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not on the elements.

577
00:34:25,525 --> 00:34:28,047
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you getting, um...

578
00:34:29,813 --> 00:34:35,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Relaxed, in terms of stopping what you're doing, they were tiring you, it's fine.

579
00:34:36,694 --> 00:34:44,176
[SPEAKER_01]: But you getting relaxed, or taking that relaxation then on the level of becoming unaware in your mind, because you worked so hard, that's not fine.

580
00:34:44,776 --> 00:34:45,816
[SPEAKER_01]: That bit is not fine.

581
00:34:46,956 --> 00:34:51,557
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like you overly welcoming it now, and put... and doing it on the level where it shouldn't be done.

582
00:34:51,997 --> 00:34:52,577
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it is.

583
00:34:52,897 --> 00:34:56,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean you must now contemplate dhamma furiously.

584
00:34:57,313 --> 00:34:57,473
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

585
00:34:57,793 --> 00:34:58,994
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to do that either.

586
00:34:59,494 --> 00:35:01,514
[SPEAKER_01]: But you mustn't give to unawareness.

587
00:35:03,835 --> 00:35:09,397
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you... sometimes it's, you know, it's even in the sutras Buddha said, yeah, you might be making too much effort to figure things out.

588
00:35:10,297 --> 00:35:15,859
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you drop the effort to figure things out, but it doesn't mean you drop your awareness of your situation.

589
00:35:15,879 --> 00:35:16,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, alertness.

590
00:35:16,959 --> 00:35:18,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that is effortless.

591
00:35:20,200 --> 00:35:24,041
[SPEAKER_01]: But being in the present moment is effortless, because whether you want it or not, you're in the present moment.

592
00:35:24,161 --> 00:35:25,382
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a matter of choice.

593
00:35:26,330 --> 00:35:28,451
[SPEAKER_01]: Recognition of the world of bodies, it's not a matter.

594
00:35:28,731 --> 00:35:29,952
[SPEAKER_01]: You already have that knowledge.

595
00:35:30,832 --> 00:35:31,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you seated now?

596
00:35:32,513 --> 00:35:33,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, let me, let me...

597
00:35:33,494 --> 00:35:34,634
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll get back to you on that one.

598
00:35:35,134 --> 00:35:35,975
[SPEAKER_01]: You already know.

599
00:35:37,195 --> 00:35:38,376
[SPEAKER_01]: It's effortless, that knowledge.

600
00:35:38,396 --> 00:35:41,638
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to be attending and re-attending and recollect.

601
00:35:41,698 --> 00:35:42,438
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already there.

602
00:35:43,359 --> 00:35:49,121
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is stop distracting yourself from it by making choices on the kind of sensuality aversion and distraction and delusion.

603
00:35:51,623 --> 00:35:53,864
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the effortless mindfulness is not done by you.

604
00:35:54,769 --> 00:35:59,612
[SPEAKER_01]: The effortless mindfulness is basically created by you not distracting yourself from it.

605
00:35:59,893 --> 00:36:00,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

606
00:36:00,453 --> 00:36:01,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Not turning away from it.

607
00:36:01,374 --> 00:36:02,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Not turning away from it.

608
00:36:02,414 --> 00:36:02,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

609
00:36:03,455 --> 00:36:06,717
[SPEAKER_00]: Not consciously going into oblivion, oblivious state of... Yeah.

610
00:36:06,737 --> 00:36:06,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

611
00:36:07,858 --> 00:36:10,880
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why greed, aversion, delusion and non-greed, non-aversion...

612
00:36:11,060 --> 00:36:14,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, the Buddha said, he won't think.

613
00:36:14,843 --> 00:36:15,744
[SPEAKER_01]: He won't entertain.

614
00:36:15,924 --> 00:36:18,545
[SPEAKER_01]: He will not welcome and sort of cherish.

615
00:36:19,406 --> 00:36:22,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything that's rooted in non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion, he will.

616
00:36:23,210 --> 00:36:24,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Tireless energy.

617
00:36:24,431 --> 00:36:25,112
[SPEAKER_01]: No need to rest.

618
00:36:25,332 --> 00:36:25,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Day and night.

619
00:36:27,914 --> 00:36:29,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's it's tireless.

620
00:36:29,755 --> 00:36:32,297
[SPEAKER_01]: It's nothing to do with that which tires you, which is the senses.

621
00:36:37,762 --> 00:36:41,425
[SPEAKER_00]: But either way, it's going to be work.

622
00:36:42,245 --> 00:36:43,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you need to build it up.

623
00:36:43,786 --> 00:36:44,527
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to build it up.

624
00:36:47,040 --> 00:36:52,463
[SPEAKER_00]: It's almost like this fear to even just even begin a little bit, not even... Yeah, yeah, of course.

625
00:36:52,503 --> 00:36:52,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

626
00:36:53,044 --> 00:36:54,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's horrible.

627
00:36:54,885 --> 00:36:58,867
[SPEAKER_01]: In the beginning, when... you're just bewildered.

628
00:36:59,888 --> 00:37:03,090
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you just have to... it's just... it's worse than the physical work.

629
00:37:04,170 --> 00:37:05,131
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not incidental.

630
00:37:05,151 --> 00:37:07,812
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole humanity tends to turn away from it.

631
00:37:08,433 --> 00:37:13,676
[SPEAKER_01]: Turn away from responsibility, from reason, turn away from rational thinking, recognition.

632
00:37:14,936 --> 00:37:15,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Takes effort.

633
00:37:16,247 --> 00:37:24,038
[SPEAKER_01]: It takes enduring, it takes pain, it takes some greater reason to make you go into that direction of more resistance against the grain.

634
00:37:25,660 --> 00:37:27,262
[SPEAKER_01]: And most people won't do that.

